import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(4,3,"The Mind through the Lens of the Framework, Part One: Every Moment Is a Meeting","Next time a moment lands hard, separate what actually arrived from what you brought to it. How much was the second?",[
mc("U","What does Conversation 4.3 mean by saying that every moment is a meeting?",
 ["The mind meets other minds, and each moment is a conversation between two.","What you live is built from what is there and what you bring to it.","The senses meet the world raw, and the mind adds its comments afterwards.","Past and future meet in the present, and the mind lives where they cross."],1,
 "You never perceive the world raw. Each moment meets the real signal with your filter (the grooves of your past), your attention (where it is tuned and how hard it grips) and a standing tilt of me and mine. What you live is the meeting, which is why the same event lands in two worlds for two people.",
 "A moment is the meeting of what is there and what you bring","Part 1"),
mc("A","After a hard moment, a woman splits it into what actually happened and what she brought to it. What does Conversation 4.3 say this often does?",
 ["It proves that the other person was in the wrong, which settles the matter.","It loosens the grip by half, when she sees how much of it was her own filter.","It removes her feelings, so that she can go on without any reaction.","It shows that her feelings were false, and that she should ignore them in future."],1,
 "Seeing the seam between the two halves is the practice. Naming that this is mostly what I brought is often enough to loosen the grip by half. It changes the morning rush without needing to change the world.",
 "Naming what you brought often loosens the grip by half","Part 1"),
mc("A","A man wants to change how a hard moment lands, and asks which part of the meeting he can move right now. What does Conversation 4.3 say?",
 ["The filter, which can be rebuilt by one good decision.","The noise floor, which can be switched off when a person chooses.","Attention, both where it is tuned and how hard it grips.","The real signal, which a person can change by looking at it differently."],2,
 "The filter is slow work, the noise floor is the static of an unsettled system, and the signal is what is there. Of all the parts, attention is the one you can move in the moment: where it is tuned, and how hard it grips (the gain, set largely through the body and breath).",
 "Attention is the part that can be moved in the moment","Part 1"),
judge("“Each part of the meeting, the signal, the filter, the attention, the gain and the noise, comes in one texture only, and reading a moment means finding which label fits.”",F,
 "Every part carries all three textures at once, heavy and stuck, restless, or clear and settled, in a shifting mix. So reading a moment is never one label such as I am tamasic. It is reading which texture dominates the filter, the gain and the noise right now.",
 "Every part holds all three textures in a mix; read the mix","Part 1"),
mc("A","A teacher is half-present in a lesson, managing and monitoring. What does Conversation 4.3 suggest she do?",
 ["Monitor more closely, so that nothing in the room escapes her.","Drop the monitoring and come into time with the actual room, tuning in on purpose.","Ignore the room altogether and carry on with the plan, as if she were alone.","Wait until the lesson ends, and then think over what went wrong."],1,
 "The flicker is what the clarity layer feels like when your rhythm and the world’s do not match. As attention tunes in and the tilt of me softens, the flicker settles into flow. Catch the wobble, and then tune in on purpose.",
 "Catch the flicker, then tune in to the room on purpose","Part 2"),
mc("U","What happens at the limit of tuning, when the tilt of me is gone, according to Conversation 4.3?",
 ["The mind goes blank, and nothing at all is known by the person.","Knower and known fuse: the object alone shining, called samādhi.","The person loses all feeling, and ordinary life becomes impossible to lead.","The person gains an answer to every question, and no more thought is ever needed."],1,
 "At the limit, tuning is perfect and the tilt is gone, and the two fuse into one: no knower here and known there, just the thing itself. The contemplative traditions call that complete meeting samādhi, not a blank trance but the clearest possible knowing.",
 "At the limit knower and known fuse: samādhi, the clearest knowing","Part 2"),
mc("A","A man keeps churning over an argument, and the harder he grips it the tighter it gets. Which step fits Conversation 4.3?",
 ["Push harder at the thought, since a firmer grip will end it sooner.","See that he is pushing the wheel, then step off: slow breaths and what is in front of him.","Distract himself with something exciting until the argument has been forgotten.","Argue the other side in his head until he is sure of who is right."],1,
 "Trying to control the mind by force is doubling: pushing an already spinning wheel harder, which churns the duality you want to escape. The other kind, trebling, changes the level. The breath is the accessible first step.",
 "Pushing the wheel tightens it; stepping off changes the level","Part 3"),
judge("“The breath alone carries a person across: once the churn is calm, nothing more is needed.”",P,
 "The breath is real and is the accessible first step, which is why clinics use it for anxiety and the contemplative traditions begin with it. But it calms the water and no more. The lasting lift is steadying attention to a single point, ekāgratā, and through that waking the discerning mind, buddhi, to resonate with the Self.",
 "The breath is the first step; one-pointed attention carries across","Part 3"),
mc("A","A patient says, “The harder I try to stop the loop, the worse it gets.” What does Conversation 4.3 suggest a doctor show her?",
 ["That she needs to try harder, since the loop will yield to enough effort.","That she is pushing the wheel, which has an off: one slow breath, then something present.","That the loop is only in her mind, and so it is not worth taking seriously at all.","That the loop is her nature, and she should learn to live beside it for good."],1,
 "Dr Watson’s practice is to show the patient that the harder she pushes, the tighter it gets, and then offer the smallest step off: one slow breath, attention to something actually present. It is not solving the loop; it is showing that the wheel has an off.",
 "Show that the wheel has an off: one breath and something present","Part 3"),
judge("“The instrument that makes the storm is the same instrument that can step out of it.”",T,
 "Swamiji asks Dr Watson to keep holding this thought: it is the same mind that pushes the wheel and that can step off it. The reading is meant to be hopeful and practical, not a verdict.",
 "The mind that makes the storm can also step out of it","Part 3")])
